div.ab:
Chapter V. 6:11 And the angel of the Lord came and sat under the Terebinth (pistacio-terebinthuis) which is in afra i.e., in Afrath. 6:11 The village of Ya ash the father of Azri. i.e., and of Gideon. 6:15 With what (means) shall I save Israel, for behold my clan with Pethaha of Beth is small in Manassah. 6:19 And the meat he put in a basket, and the strained liquid he placed in a pint-pot. Gr., and the soup he shall put in a pot. 6:22 And Gideon said O! Lord God. Gr., Ha-ha-ha Lord, Lord! 6:31 he who strives before him before morning shall die. Gr., He who contends for him shall die before the morning. i.e., he who was an adversary to him and destroyed his altar. 6:32 And he called him i.e., Ya ash named his son Gideon. in that day Nedhuv el i.e., 6:32 Let the Baal contend with him. Gr. says Iruv el with Yadh and Resh, and it is not correct. 6:37 And if there should be dewon the fleece alone, and on all the ground dryness, I shall know that you will save Israel by my hands as you said. 6:38 And it was so. i.e., the type of goodness that was taken away(lifted) from the Midianites and given to Gideon. 6:39 I shall try another time 6:39 May there be dryness upon the fleece alone, and upon all the land may there be dew. i.e., Type of goodness which were to the children of Israel, (
) through Gideon. 7:1 And
they encamped at the spring of Hadhor 7:1 on the north of the hill of Romtha. 7:2 Lest Israel should glory and say my hand has given me victory i.e., it has saved me.

Chapter VII. 8:11 On the east of Navah and of Yavg ha 8:14 And the chiefs of Sakkuth wrote to him i.e., each one in his own name (meaning separately) that upon them he should inflict punishment for reproaching him. 8:18 What manner of
men are those you have murdered in Tavur? with Pethaha of Qof. 8:21 And Gideon rose up and murdered Zavah and Salmena. with Pethaha of Qof. 8:21 And he took the crescents which were on the necks of their camels. i.e., ornaments of silver shaped in the form of the moon. 8:27 And Gideon took from them and made an idol. i.e., an image of a mighty man. 8:27 And set it up in his village ofra i.e., he did not make it for worship, but as a memorial of his victory. But it was a stumbling block to his house and to his people. (
) 9:9 And am going to wave over the trees Gr., and go to be head over the woods. 9:14 And all the trees said to the bramble-tree (ar. ) you rule over us i.e., unto the bramble tree, Yutham the son of Gideon, likens Abi-malekh his brother from the concubine, by reason of his smallness, absence of goodness in him, and his childishness. 9:15 Otherwise a fire shall come out from the bramble, and shall eat the cedars of Lebanon i.e., from Abi-malik, and it shall destroy the seventy good children of Gideon in whom the children of Shekim found no pleasure.

Chapter XII. And he burnt of the stacks (heaps) and even the standing corn. Gr., of both the stubble and the standing. 15:8 And he went down and abode in the cleft in the crag of atmin i.e., because Samson had smitten the Philistines from their shin-bone to their loins (cf. 14:8) it was called Sheqifa (the crag. Lit., the battered) 15:13 And they bound him with two new chains Gr., new ropes. 15:14 And when he arrived at Lih (
) 15:15 And he found a hard jaw of a donkey with Pethaha of Teth. i.e., still moist and not easy to break it. Gr., of a donkey which is thrown away. 15:19 And the Lord God rend the curb of the jaw of the donkey. Gr., and God opened the hollow of the jaw, and there came out of it water and he drank. i.e., if it were not that everything which is pure to those who are clean, how is it then that it was not from a rock but from a dead carcass God fed Samson honey, and from the jaw bone of a donkey he gave him water to drink? Then Samson went to azath Gr., Gaza. 16:4 And after that he loved a
woman in the valley of Saruq and her name was Delilah. 16:6 If you may be diminished (in strength) with a vowelless Beth. 16:7 If they should bind me with seven moist strings that have not dried up i.e., that are not hard. Gr., with seven wet sinews that are not ruined. 16:13 If you intertwine seven locks of my head with the web. Gr., If you weave seven plaits of my head unto the web. 16:14 And he plucked away the loom of the weavers i.e., weavers 16:17 And he uncovered to her all his heart i.e., by desertion from God he revealed his type (mystery) to the harlot, that he might know to what baseness the unlawfulness was drawing him. 16:17 If I were shaved then my strength will depart from me i.e., If my covenant with God ceases I become mean (base).

Chapter XVI. 20:33 And they put themselves in array Be el Tamar. 20:38 And there was an appointed time between the men of Israel and the liers-in-wait with full pronunciation of
He. Gr., and there was a covenant. 20:40 And behold the thick smoke of the village was ascending to heaven. Gr., the end (finish) of the city. 20:48 And the children of Israel returned against the children of Benjamin and smote them with the edge of the sword. i.e., After they fasted and burnt offerings and whole-sacrifices, God gave them victory (cf. 20:26). 21:7 That we swore by the Lord with Hevasa of Mim. 21:14 And he gave to them the women whom they kept alive from among the women of Yavish Gil adh, but were not sufficient for them i.e., the children of Benjamin were not blotted out completely as the Sadomites because their sin was milder than theirs. 21:19 To the south of Lavuna 21:20 Go (you Pl.) lay in wait in the vineyards. with Pethaha of Kof. Nest with Pethaha of Mim. 21:21 And when you see the daughters (
) of Shila had gone out to play the tambourines Gr., to dance in the dances. 21:23 And they took women for all of themselves i.e., the children of Benjamin from among the beaters of the tambourines whom they had seized. Gr., from among those that were dancing.
